'''''Jabalisaurus''''' is an extinct genus of [[ophthalmosaurid]] [[Ichthyosauria|ichthyosaurs]] known from [[Late Jurassic]] rocks of Mexico and Germany. The genus contains two species. The [[type species]], '''''Jabalisaurus meztli''''', was described in 2021 based on a single specimen from the [[La Casita Formation]] of Mexico.<ref name="J.meztli21">{{Cite journal|last1=Barrientos-Lara|first1=Jair Israel|last2=Alvarado-Ortega|first2=Jesús|date=2021-11-01|title=A new ophthalmosaurid (Ichthyosauria) from the Upper Kimmeridgian deposits of the La Casita Formation, near Gómez Farías, Coahuila, northern Mexico|url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S089598112100345X|journal=Journal of South American Earth Sciences|language=en|volume=111|article-number=103499|doi=10.1016/j.jsames.2021.103499|bibcode=2021JSAES.11103499B|issn=0895-9811|url-access=subscription}}</ref> It had initially been identified as ''[[Ophthalmosaurus]]'' cf. ''icenicus''.<ref>Buchy, M.-C. 2010. "First record of ''Ophthalmosaurus'' (Reptilia, Ichthyosauria) from the Tithonian (Upper Jurassic) of Mexico". ''Journal of Paleontology''. '''84'''(1): 149–155</ref> A second species, '''''Jabalisaurus tethyensis''''', was described in 2026 based on three specimens from the [[Altmühltal Formation]] of Germany. One of these, JME-SOS-08369, is a complete, articulated skeleton encased in soft tissue.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Maxwell |first1=E. E. |last2=Neumann |first2=J. |last3=Serafini |first3=G. |year=2026 |title=Late Jurassic ichthyosaurs from southern Germany |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=29 |issue=2 |article-number=29.2.a30 |doi=10.26879/1676 |url=https://palaeo-electronica.org/content/2026/5919-late-jurassic-ichthyosaurs-from-germany |doi-access=free }}</ref> It was described previously in 2022 and identified as ''[[Aegirosaurus]]'' sp.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Delsett |first=Lene L. |last2=Friis |first2=Henrik |last3=Kölbl-Ebert |first3=Martina |last4=Hurum |first4=Jørn H. |date=April 7, 2022 |title=The soft tissue and skeletal anatomy of two Late Jurassic ichthyosaur specimens from the Solnhofen archipelago |journal=PeerJ |language=en |volume=10 |article-number=e13173 |doi=10.7717/peerj.13173 |issn=2167-8359 |pmc=8995021 |pmid=35415019 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

